# Go's ratconv_test.go, translated. fmt.Fscanf(r, "%v", x) is
# x.scan!(reader, "v"). Inputs marked "long:" in Go's float64inputs run
# only with Go's -long flag and are skipped here too.

import "strconv"
import "math"

test "ScanExponent" {
  for a in t_exponent_tests {
    r = new_byte_reader(a.s)
    x, b, err = scan_exponent_raw(r, a.base2ok, a.sep_ok)
    assert_eq err, a.err
    assert_eq x, a.x
    assert_eq b, a.b
    next_: I64 = 0
    if ch = r.read_byte! {
      next_ = ch.to_i64
    }
    assert_eq next_, a.next_
  }
}

test "RatSetString" {
  for t in t_set_string_tests + t_set_string_tests2 {
    x = Rat.new
    if v = x.set_string!(t.in_) {
      assert t.ok, "SetString(#{t.in_}) expected failure"
      assert_eq v.rat_string, t.out if t.ok
    } else {
      assert !t.ok, "SetString(#{t.in_}) expected success"
    }
  }
}

test "RatSetStringZero" {
  got = rv("0")
  want = Rat.new
  want.set_int64!(0)
  assert got == want, "SetString(\"0\") == SetInt64(0)"
}

test "RatScan" {
  for t in t_set_string_tests {
    x = Rat.new
    r = x.scan!(new_byte_reader(t.in_), "v")
    assert r.ok? == t.ok, "#{t.in_}: ok should be #{t.ok}"
    assert_eq x.rat_string, t.out if r.ok?
  }
}

test "FloatString" {
  for t in t_float_string_tests {
    x = rv(t.in_)
    assert_eq x.float_string(t.prec), t.out
  }
}

def special_cases(f32: Bool) -> Bool {
  for input in t_float64inputs {
    next if input.start_with?("long:")
    x = Rat.new
    rr = x.set_string!(input)
    assert rr.some?, "Rat.SetString(#{input}) failed"
    r = rr || Rat.new
    f = 0.0
    exact = false
    if f32 {
      f, exact = r.float32
    } else {
      f, exact = r.float64
    }
    if !input.include?("/") {
      e = 0.0
      if f32 {
        e = strconv.parse_float_bits(input, 32).unwrap_or(0.0)
        e = strconv.parse_float_bits(input, 32).err.some? ? parse_float_inf(input, 32) : e
      } else {
        e = strconv.parse_float_bits(input, 64).unwrap_or(0.0)
        e = strconv.parse_float_bits(input, 64).err.some? ? parse_float_inf(input, 64) : e
      }
      same = math.float64bits(e) == math.float64bits(f) || (f == 0.0 && r.num.bit_len == 0)
      assert same, "strconv.parse_float(#{input}) = #{e}, want #{f}"
    }
    next if !is_finite(f)
    assert best_approx?(f, r, f32), "(input was #{input})"
    assert non_lossy_roundtrip?(f, f32), "roundtrip #{input}"
    was_exact = rat_of_float(f).cmp(r) == 0
    assert_eq exact, was_exact
  }
  true
}

# Go's ParseFloat result on a range error: ±Inf (or ±0 for underflow,
# which alx's parse_float_bits reports as a value, not an error).
def parse_float_inf(s: Str, _bits: I64) -> F64 {
  return math.inf(-1) if s.start_with?("-")
  math.inf(1)
}

test "Float32SpecialCases" {
  assert special_cases(true), "float32"
}

test "Float64SpecialCases" {
  assert special_cases(false), "float64"
}

test "Issue31184" {
  x = Rat.new
  for want in t_test_issue31184_0 {
    x.set_string!(want)
    assert_eq x.float_string(3), want
  }
}

test "Issue45910" {
  x = Rat.new
  for t in t_test_issue45910_0 {
    assert_eq x.set_string!(t.input).some?, t.want
  }
}

test "FloatPrec" {
  for t in t_test_float_prec_0 {
    f = Rat.new
    if t.f != "zero" {
      assert f.set_string!(t.f).some?, "invalid test case #{t.f}"
    }
    fdec = t.fdec
    for _i in 0...2 {
      prec, ok = f.float_prec
      assert prec == t.prec && ok == t.ok, "#{t.f}: FloatPrec(#{f}): got #{prec}, #{ok}"
      assert_eq f.float_string(t.prec), fdec
      if f.sign > 0 {
        f.neg!(f)
        fdec = "-" + fdec
      }
    }
  }
}
